WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.400
As one of the most ambitious crossover events
in history, Avengers: Infinity War featured

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.900
a miles-long cast roster that brought together
all of the corners of the sprawling Marvel

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.030
Cinematic Universe.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:15.450
Black Widow, Black Panther, Iron Man, the
Guardians of the Galaxy — the whole crime-fighting

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.450
crew was there!

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:20.140
Well, except for Hawkeye, Ant-Man, and, for
the most of the movie, the Hulk.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.050
In the first few minutes of Infinity War,
Thanos defeats the Hulk during a fight on

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.880
the refugee ship that fled from Asgard at
the end of Thor: Ragnarok.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.610
The not-so-jolly green giant didn't appear
in the film again after that— and we finally

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:33.710
know why.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:37.870
According to the commentary track from the
digital release of Avengers: Infinity War,

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:39.280
the Hulk wasn't M.I.A.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.940
because he was ashamed that Thanos pummeled
him to the ground.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.070
It's actually even more twisted than that:
The Hulk is getting sick of coming to Banner's

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:50.079
aid every time he and the Avengers need assistance
in the form of brute force.

00:00:50.079 --> 00:00:51.520
"I have an army…"

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.960
"We have a hulk."

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.550
Joe Russo explained:

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:58.440
"People speculated whether there was some
fear on the Hulk's part about having to face

00:00:58.440 --> 00:00:59.550
Thanos again.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:03.390
But I think ultimately it's that he's tired
of playing hero to Bruce Banner."

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:08.030
It's easy to see why viewers would rally behind
the idea that the Hulk was in some way emotionally

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.190
bruised after his scuffle with Thanos.

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.670
The Hulk staying in hiding because he was
feeling fear?

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:16.420
That's not exactly a common occurrence when
it comes to our temperamental green-skinned

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.820
buddy — his whole schtick is getting angry
and jumping into a fight, after all.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.340
"Hulk, stop!

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.040
Just for once in your life don't smash …

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.740
"Big monster!"

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.790
Fear is the opposite of what the Hulk is about,
so it's only natural to latch onto something

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:33.790
so rare.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:37.860
The Hulk experiencing an emotion he's rarely
felt before and not fully understanding how

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.840
to process it is a solid theory that makes
for a satisfying character arc.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.070
But the real reason for the Hulk's absence
is way more fulfilling, for two reasons.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:50.890
It gives Banner's alter-ego the autonomy to
become more than just the muscular manifestation

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:55.689
of Banner's dark inner disquiet, and it pushes
Banner to stop relying on the Hulk to save

00:01:55.689 --> 00:01:57.219
him from sticky situations.

00:01:57.219 --> 00:02:01.410
While many Marvel fans have believed Banner
to be constantly trying to hold the Hulk in,

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:05.159
it looks like the tables are beginning to
turn as the Hulk has finally put his huge

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:06.610
green foot down.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:08.720
Russo elaborated on that dynamic even more:

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.190
"There's a host body that is being fought
over by two distinct personalities who hate

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:14.190
each other.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:18.250
[...] We thought an interesting direction
to take him in is, well, what if Banner, who

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:22.890
typically uses the Hulk to solve crisis situations,
what if the Hulk were no longer interested

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.070
in solving those problems for Banner?

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:28.250
So, the relationship is becoming increasingly
dysfunctional..."

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.780
Russo's explanation here shoots down all other
possible reasons as to why Banner couldn't

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:37.530
turn into the Hulk in Infinity War — including
the suggestion that he was too angry to transform

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.800
or that he blamed himself for what happened
on the Asgardian ship.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.840
But it also stamps out one of the more thought-provoking
theories surrounding the character.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:49.280
Another unusual fan theory suggested that
the Hulk was missing from Infinity War because

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.650
Loki was actually masquerading as Banner.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.230
That would certainly explain why there weren't
any "Hulk out" moments.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:03:01.160
But given that Russo goes on to focus specifically
on Banner and the Hulk's tense relationship,

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.540
that theory effectively falls apart as well.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.440
After the jaw-dropping ending of Avengers:
Infinity War, we've been operating under the

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.060
impression that Avengers 4 will rip out more
hearts and feature more intensity than previously

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.060
thought possible.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:17.410
Now, it looks like Bruce Banner and the Hulk's
turbulent dynamic will be a major part of

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:18.410
that chaos.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:22.080
"Haha, you guys are screwed now."

